WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
Pharos turns a phone report into a shared response workflow. Beacon keeps reported urgency, service area, and human verification visible in one responder workspace.

2
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:13.000
First, a real call with fictional scenario details.

call-excerpt
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:24.000
[Real phone-call excerpt with fictional scenario details]

3
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:37.000
After the call, Beacon shows its completed session and generated speaker transcript. Responders can inspect the caller's exact words and open the linked case.

4
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:50.000
The case preserves location, needs, and priority explanations. New reports can update urgency while retaining earlier history. Suggested ranking supports human decisions; it does not dispatch help.

5
00:00:50.000 --> 00:01:01.000
The training lab accepts a prepared fictional transcript. One action processes it into a persistent case, making the intake workflow easy to rehearse without a phone call.

6
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:14.000
Content concerns appear beside reported danger. Normal assignment and arrival estimates stay on hold until a human reviews the current evidence. Concern signals are not proof of deception.

7
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:27.000
Here, the reviewer records a reason and accepts the fictional report. An eligible responder can then claim ownership. Decisions remain tied to the evidence version and audit history.

8
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:39.000
The desk explicitly reports On the way and enters a fresh estimate. Claiming alone never confirms travel, and elapsed time never confirms arrival. Updated facts can require reassessment.

9
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:49.000
Coverage profiles show service areas, capabilities, availability, and capacity. New profiles require coordinator approval before they can receive cases.

10
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:57.000
The product includes a user manual, test specification, and system design. Human responders own the final decisions.

